Benthic Kayō
Ingredients: J-POP + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Synth Bass  
Creative Catalyst: It was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a buried municipal time capsule.

Benthic Kayō sounds like a lost 1986 Japanese idol single submerged for forty years in freezing coastal brine, its sugar-rush vocal hooks dissolving into suspended vapor while an immense, tectonic synth bass drags the floor beneath it. 

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 78–88 BPM. The pulse is dragging, tidal, and unquantized, mimicking the slow, uneven warble of waterlogged capstan motors fighting gravity.
- Harmonic Palette: Sweet, hyper-melodic J-Pop major-seventh and suspended pentatonic chord progressions drowned in microtonal pitch drift, cavernous plate reverberation, and saline tape decay.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Electrolytic Tape-Leaching: Master stems are transferred onto corroded 1/4-inch ferric tape while immersed in chilled saline solution, causing oxide particles to shed physically over the playhead and creating spontaneous micro-dropouts.
  2. Benthos Sub-Ducking: Ultra-low analog synth bass (30–55 Hz) sidechains the entire top end, causing sparkling bell-chimes and airy, whisper-doubled idol harmonies to violently submerge whenever the bass triggers.
  3. Corrupted Idol Choruses: Hyper-produced J-pop chorus vocal runs are deliberately clipped, pitch-bent upward by a quarter-tone, and smeared through diffuse multi-tap delays until syllables bleed into sheer oceanic wind.
- Dynamics: Songs don't build through crescendo; they shift like barometric pressure. Pristine, airy vocal melodies drift unaccompanied in cavernous space until catastrophic, heavy-weight synth bass lines drop without warning, pulling the music from floating bliss into crushing marine depth.

Scene & Origin Lore
In 2024, municipal workers unearthing a 1985 time capsule beneath the silted docks of Shimizu Port discovered an unmarked TDK cassette encased in cracked acrylic. Seawater had seeped through the casing, semi-erasing a master recording from an uncredited idol unit while fusing the magnetic oxide into strange, sub-aquatic harmonics. When an underground collective of Tokyo producers digitised the tape, they became obsessed with the artifacts: the sugary, breathy vocals preserved in amber atop a swollen, terrifying low-end rumble. The resulting movement—dubbed Benthic Kayō—spawned clandestine harbor raves in abandoned fish-packing warehouses. Followers dress in "oxide couture": salt-stained school blazers, translucent industrial fishing aprons, bleached sea-silk ribbons, and portable modified Walkmans tethered like life-support rigs.

Typical Instruments: Roland System-100M (jaw-rattling sub-bass), Yamaha DX7 (corrupted crystalline electric pianos and chime patches), Roland VP-330 (ghostly vocal morphs), reel-to-reel tape decks, contact microphones dipped in saltwater tanks, airy female lead vocals stacked in four-part micro-choral layers.

For Fans Of: Cocteau Twins, Meiko Nakahara, early Grimes, Oneohtrix Point Never.

